      "problem": "If \n$W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -6 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]$, then $\\text{rank}(W)=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the rank of $H,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -6 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(W)=3.$",
